+| Alias / PRE / vectorize / unroll / scalar passes; IR/lowering; live ranges; all four backends | Existing local implementations diverge extensively and include local safety fixes. Path presence is not semantic proof. No unvalidated hunk from these broad families is transplanted. | Preserve local state; exact affected paths remain in the inventory. |
+| Workload benchmark sources | Many incoming programs already exist verbatim or have locally extended versions. Local gzip/zlib-ng/expat/SQLite/Linux/glibc extracts have their own provenance. | Reuse existing corpus, not a duplicate historical benchmark tree. |
+| Website, license bundle, reports, `.claude` settings, task deletions | No demonstrated incremental code-generation value; license changes require their own provenance/legal review. | Not imported. |
+
+Reproduce the dead-prototype check with:
+
+```sh
+git grep -n 'allocate_machinsts\|rewrite_machinsts\|collect_fixed_intervals' a9987d0 -- src
+```
+
+## VM-safe evidence, not imaginary performance counters
+
+Host: KVM, two logical CPUs, advertised Xeon model 106 (not Raptor Lake),
+~1.9 GiB RAM; **6 GiB installed and active swap** (2 + 4 GiB files). Swap was
+actually used while building. Largest writable filesystem: root, initially
+~21 GiB free. Build/toolchain scratch resides on that filesystem outside the
+small delivery workspace; validated source and patches are archived under
+`/home/user/artifacts` before scratch can be lost.
+
+Compiler build: `scripts/build_lccc_fast.sh`, Rust stable 1.99.0,
+`fastbuild`, **opt-level=1, jobs=2**, `CARGO_PROFILE_FASTBUILD_DEBUG=0`,
+`CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` to bound this cold build's memory. Local reference GCC
+14.2.0; local build linker bfd 2.44. These are not mislabeled as GCC 16.2 or
+binutils 2.47. `perf` is not installed; **no PMU samples, Raptor Lake claims,
+or timing/geomean wins** are asserted.
+
+### Workload correctness and exact generated-binary non-regression
+
+Run before and after using the existing strict runner, `--opt=-O2 --skip-perf`:
+
+| Kernel | GCC-matched output, both builds | Before/after LCCC binary |
+|---|---|---|
+| gzip CRC32 | `372e56ab` | byte-identical |
+| zlib-ng Adler32 | `8c331ae0` | byte-identical |
+| Expat XML scanner | `626766774715194881` | byte-identical |
+| SQLite varint | `deedcdd4edc1c0f1` | byte-identical |
+| Linux find-bit | `0` | byte-identical |
+| glibc memcmp | `2158787064` | byte-identical |
+
+For these **six extracted kernels**, byte identity is stronger evidence of
+unchanged generated code than noisy VM timing. It does not prove unchanged
+compile time, whole-library behavior or untested code shapes. Raw hashes are
+in [`evidence/a9987d0-workload-identity.json`](evidence/a9987d0-workload-identity.json).
+The existing `archpkgbuilds/packages` selectors were checked; the requested
+kernel families already exist, so no redundant source extraction was added.
+
+### Known cost outside those six kernels: guarded CLZ in `bitops`
+
+A final assembly red-team check of the incoming/common `bitops.c` benchmark
+found a **real static cost**, not binary identity: its already-zero-guarded CLZ
+now has two extra instructions (`movq` and `orq $1`) before `lzcntl`. The six
+identical workload binaries above must not be generalized to this benchmark.
+The portable zero-case repair is necessary, but a cheap dominating-nonzero
+proof could retain the old sequence for guarded calls. That optimization is
+not implemented here. No runtime regression percentage is asserted from static
+counts or timing concurrent with CI. This cost is explicitly accepted for the
+correctness repair and prioritized in the follow-up, rather than concealed by
+a geometric mean. Reproduce by comparing `-O2 -S` output for
+`tests/benchmark/programs/bitops.c` with the baseline and submitted compiler.
+
+### Active Compiler Explorer comparison
+
+Existing `scripts/codegen_oracle.py` (which reuses `godbolt.py`), equal flags
+`-O2 -march=x86-64-v3`; full assembly is retained in the evidence JSON. These
+are **static whole-function instruction counts**, including return, not cycles:
+
+| Function | LCCC after | GCC 16.2 | Clang 23.1.0 | ICC 2021.10.0 | ICX latest |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| canonical `swar_full` | 3 | 3 | 2 | 16 | 16 |
+| truncated `swar_low16` | 18 | 16 | 16 | 17 | 16 |
+
+The canonical LCCC function is unchanged by the fix. Its return-register move
+is a follow-up opportunity versus Clang, not a proven runtime loss. The former
+three-instruction **wrong** implementation of `swar_low16` is not a performance
+baseline. More instructions to restore C semantics are mandatory, not a
+regression to hide. ICX `latest` is a moving compiler ID; the captured response
+metadata, not an invented release number, identifies this run.
+
+Linker oracle preferences already match the requested policy:
+`tools/linker/setup_oracles.sh` pins bfd **2.47** and mold **2.42.1**, using
+`-DMOLD_TARGETS='X86_64;I386'`. The version guard rejects stale binaries and
+failed `--version` commands. No gratuitous edit or linker rebuild is included
+for a non-linker defect. The fast mirror provisions its own pinned GAS/objdump 2.47 pairs; that
+assembler/disassembler validation is distinct from building or benchmarking the
+bfd/mold **linkers**, which this audit does not do.
+
+## Acceptance and continuity
+
+Reproducible final local gate (fast only):
+
+```sh
+CARGO_PROFILE_FASTBUILD_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 CI_LOCAL_JOBS=2 \
+  bash scripts/ci_local.sh --fast
+```
+
+The final pass/failure, gate counts and tested tree are recorded in the
+session's `artifacts/ci-fast.log` and delivery receipt. A normal final
+`scripts/lccc-snapshot.sh` publication requires a matching fast/full pass
+stamp; interim saves use the explicit **UNGATED** label and are not passed off
+as final validation. Slow gates, full parent-project builds, a full incoming
+compiler build and hardware performance experiments are outside this audit's
+validation scope. See the companion follow-up for unresolved research work.
